{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdLcAhrqgyHxmsYv5Zet","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote all three files for the repair attempt, and the self-check prints `OK`. It doesn't test novelty, so I can't confirm this version clears the near-duplicate gate that rejected the last one.\n\n- **Model:** `side_bias_asymmetric_tolerance_stretch`. It refines `personal_side_bias_lopsided_stretch` (rank 2), where each person leans toward the left or right button by their own amount, drawn from a population that may lean left overall. The one change is borrowing the incumbent's asymmetric tolerance: switching more often than your ideal costs only a shared fraction of what switching too rarely costs. Put another way, it is the incumbent plus that button lean, which is aimed at the critique's one significant finding (people's left-choice rates vary more than the incumbent predicts, though it does not survive the FDR, q = 0.128).\n- **Why it should be different enough this time:** the rejected attempt only made the over-alternation leniency personal instead of shared, which predicted almost exactly like the incumbent (RMSE 0.0018, threshold 0.002). The new model differs from the incumbent by the overall left lean (the data's left-choice rate is 0.509), and from the side-bias model by the asymmetric tolerance. That should push it past 0.002 on the pool, but that's my estimate, not a measurement.\n- **Self-check result:** the 100-draw, single-chain smoke fit gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −3495.7, compared with the incumbent's −3500.3 from its full fit. These aren't comparable, so treat it as a sign the model works, not as a win. The check also warned that PSIS-LOO is unreliable (8.1% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance), and a single chain can't report R-hat. Admission's full fit will decide both.\n- **Caveat on fit:** adding the same kind of side bias to `most_lopsided_stretch_aversion` 